# ==============================================================================
# EXPECTED.md5 - the decode hashes the conformance gate checks against
# ==============================================================================
#
# FORMAT (pipe-separated, one decode per line, '#' starts a comment):
#
#     <vector file name>|<dav1d CLI flags>|<expected md5>
#
# The gate in linux/container-build.sh (and its Windows and macOS equivalents)
# runs, for every line:
#
#     dav1d -i test-vectors/<file> --muxer md5 -o - <flags>
#
# and requires the printed hash to equal the third field. dav1d's md5 muxer
# hashes the decoded frames themselves - every plane, row by row, Y then U then
# V, 16-bit words little-endian for high bit depth, no stride padding - so these
# hashes are a statement about the DECODED PICTURES, not about the file.
#
# WHY --filmgrain IS ON EVERY LINE
# The dav1d CLI applies film grain by default EXCEPT when the muxer is md5 or
# xxh3, where it defaults to off. Relying on that default would make the gate
# depend on a CLI convention rather than on a stated intention, so every line
# says what it wants. Only vector 05 carries grain metadata, so on the other
# five the flag makes no difference - and the pair of lines for 05 proves that.
#
# HOW THESE VALUES WERE ESTABLISHED (2026-08-28), BY TWO INDEPENDENT DECODERS
#   1. the dav1d CLI built from the vendored source by linux/build.sh (x64,
#      MODE=generate-expected), and
#   2. ffmpeg 7.1.5's own decoders, twice - once through libdav1d and once
#      through libaom-av1, which is a completely separate implementation:
#          ffmpeg -v error -c:v libdav1d   -i <vec> -pix_fmt <fmt> -f md5 -
#          ffmpeg -v error -c:v libaom-av1 -i <vec> -pix_fmt <fmt> -f md5 -
#
# All three agreed, byte for byte, on all six streams: the two md5 conventions
# turn out to hash exactly the same bytes, so ONE hash per stream serves as both
# the expected value and the cross-decoder check. (ffmpeg applies film grain by
# default, which is why its hashes match the --filmgrain 1 lines.)
#
# See README.txt in this folder for the provenance of the streams themselves and
# for how to regenerate these values.
# ==============================================================================

01-8bit-420-aom.ivf|--filmgrain 1|eacbb8abacc8bd95f290ee94e4b7fd8e
02-8bit-420-svtav1.ivf|--filmgrain 1|c38ed6ef9568f89ebe677590f8d9f8bc
03-10bit-420-aom.ivf|--filmgrain 1|76fdc38759ef4d1f863be100c49f8d7f
04-8bit-444-aom.ivf|--filmgrain 1|3fa9cbeae219dd519d44756ce44aaeb9
05-8bit-420-filmgrain-svtav1.ivf|--filmgrain 1|3d35ef826623fc31dfb7af5403833617

# The same stream with grain synthesis switched OFF. It is here so the gate
# proves that film grain is really being applied on the line above rather than
# quietly skipped: these two hashes MUST differ, and both must match.
05-8bit-420-filmgrain-svtav1.ivf|--filmgrain 0|c716ab1237c27b99976580065b7a4bd8

06-8bit-420-oddsize-keyframes-aom.ivf|--filmgrain 1|7d37c280ac0bd5dd758d1397663b4d28
